   "id": "t11-read",
   "stage": "read",
   "text": "Based on the code I just read, QAD keeps the PTQ encoding for some layers because of the **loss-based selection strategy** described in the docstring:\n\n> \"the layer keeps whichever of the PTQ initialisation and the trained state has the lower loss on held-out calibration sequences.\"\n\nHere's why this matters:\n\n1. **PTQ encoding is a starting point**: The PTQ pipeline produces initial quantization parameters (codes, 8-bit ladder parameters, rotation flags, etc.) that are already optimized for the calibration data.\n\n2. **Training may improve or degrade performance**: During QAD training, the layer is fine-tuned to minimize MSE on held-out calibration sequences. However, this training might:\n   - Improve accuracy (lower loss) \u2192 keep the trained state\n   - Degrade accuracy (higher loss) \u2192 revert to PTQ encoding\n\n3. **Selective retention**: The docstring explicitly states that \"the layer keeps whichever of the PTQ initialisation and the trained state has the lower loss on held-out calibration sequences.\" This means:\n   - If training improves the layer's performance, the trained state is kept\n   - If training degrades performance, the PTQ encoding is retained\n\nThis approach ensures that:\n- The PTQ encoding serves as a robust baseline\n- Training only modifies layers that demonstrably improve performance\n- Layers that don't benefit from training retain their PTQ encoding, avoiding unnecessary computational overhead\n\nThe key insight is that PTQ encoding is not discarded; it's kept as a fallback when training doesn't improve the layer's performance on the calibration data.",
